    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/9fe57341b631d34a9dfa41619a29f816b00b41b3b07f450fd333808230835e2c.png)\n\nThe graph of the second derivative $y={f}^{″}(x)$ of the function $f(x)$ is shown above. \nFind the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up.",
    "worked_solution": "A function $f(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={f}^{″}(x),$ we can see that ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ on the interval $(0,4)$ only. \n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is concave up on the interval $(0,4)$ only.",
